{"sequence":120,"timestamp":"2026-03-27T19:56:32.281556238Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-8-cd41c2dd","body":"In `nitrogen_cycle.rs`, the `monod_rate` helper now receives concentration-based substrate values but its doc comment and parameter name (`substrate`) give no indication of expected units. Add a brief doc comment to `monod_rate` and `monod_factor` noting that `substrate` and `k_substrate` must be in the same units (mg/L after the concentration migration) so future maintainers don't accidentally pass totals.","dedup_key":"45b6129b9cac23016e0be03d96f602303ca702c4c4262b16be6a5f99ac46ba1b","run_id":"run-20260327155320","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":121,"timestamp":"2026-03-27T19:56:32.291079421Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-8-b5b1b923","body":"The `substrate_displacement_l` function in `geometry.rs` assumes 0% substrate porosity — the entire substrate volume is subtracted from water volume. Real aquarium substrates have ~30-40% porosity, so 60-70% of the substrate column is solid. Consider adding a `porosity_index` field to `SubstrateKind` or `SubstrateLayerState` and multiplying displacement by `(1.0 - porosity)`. At minimum, add a doc comment noting the 100% displacement assumption so dependents (especially the `water_volume_l_with_substrate_depth` callers) are aware.","dedup_key":"568993310593eaa430a86d7b1b8b58d625148c013b4c21b5bf9c930ec70bd899","run_id":"run-20260327155320","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":122,"timestamp":"2026-03-27T19:56:32.300577547Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-8-91607365","body":"crates/tank_core/src/types/geometry.rs:19 still defines `TankGeometry::water_volume_l()` as gross filled volume, and crates/tank_core/src/types/geometry.rs:56 plus crates/tank_core/src/types/geometry.rs:65 build ratio helpers on that same gross basis, while crates/tank_core/src/types/state.rs:177 defines `TankState::water_volume_l()` as the new canonical net-water volume. Leaving both public APIs under the same `water_volume_l` name preserves two incompatible meanings of water volume and makes future concentration/normalization code easy to wire back to gross volume. Rename the geometry-side gross helpers or move the canonical name there so callers only have one public `water_volume_l` semantics.","dedup_key":"df8a3d332a3db63cb55612288c8ccce6f0ff49f61e27d74ae480ab5b64f1909d","run_id":"run-20260327155320","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
